I checked the disease mapping: the Radboud IDs are aligned to the Biovista disease entries.
However, in the newest Open Targets version, some diseases now appear under updated IDs (e.g., MONDO ↔ EFO, or MONDO subtypes). That’s why I highlighted both columns in yellow: one is the Radboud ID we actually store/use in disease_key_mapping.xlsx, and the other is the current Open Targets ID/name in the newest release. So the mapping is consistent, but the OT identifier may have changed upstream, which is worth noting if we want to standardize on OT IDs later. If I did not highlight, it means the names have not been changed among different versions for updates.

One more thing: we apply an ontology-hierarchy filter. If a disease has both a parent ID and one or more child (more specific) IDs in our mapping, we keep only the most specific child IDs and remove the parent term. This prevents redundancy and makes the downstream KG more precise.

If anything is not clear to you, please let me know, and I will be happy to check and reply soon!

Best regards,
Siqi


